  \Delta^2u-\beta\Delta u=\lambda u+\left(\frac{1}{|x|^\mu}*F(u)\right)f(u),
  \quad\mbox{in}\ \ \mathbb{R}^4,
  \displaystyle\int_{\mathbb{R}^4}|u|^2dx=c^2,\quad u\in H^2(\mathbb{R}^4),
  \end{array}
  \right.
  \end{split}
  \end{align*} under specific constraints and growth conditions for $\beta$, $\lambda$, and $f$, and we leverage a homotopy-based minimax principle to establish the existence of a normalized ground state solution for this equation. Additionally, we investigate the asymptotic behavior of the solution and demonstrate how it connects to a constrained variational problem. Moreover, we prove the norm estimate for the solution and show that it yields a ground state solution of the given equation. Finally, we discuss how to obtain a solution in the scalar case under relaxed conditions. Our approach builds upon previous work by Alves, Oubina, and Pucci, among others, and extends the findings in [4] to a higher dimension. To contribute to the existing literature, we establish the existence of a normalized ground state solution for a broader class of equations than those studied in [4]. To this end, we introduce a key modification to the construction of a ground state solution via a homotopy-based minimax principle, which enables us to handle a broader class of equations than those studied in [4]. This modification involves a key adjustment to the construction of a ground state solution via a homotopy-based minimax principle, which enables us to handle a broader class of equations than those studied in [4]. This modification involves a key adjustment to the construction of a ground state solution via a homotopy-based minimax principle, which enables us to handle a broader class of equations than those studied in [4]. This modification involves a key adjustment to the construction of a ground state solution via a homotopy-based minimax principle, which enables us to handle a broader class of equations than those studied in [4]. This modification involves a key adjustment to the construction of a ground state solution via

